The '''2013 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2012–13 UEFA Champions League]], the 58th season of Europe's premier club [[Association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 21st season since it was renamed from the European Champion Clubs' Cup to the [[UEFA Champions League]].

The match took place on Saturday, 25 May 2013, at [[Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]], England,<ref>{{cite news |title=New Champions League season |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0254-0d7e018b8e81-9db0cbfd5fed-1000--new-champions-league-season/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=26 June 2012 |access-date=23 May 2013 }}</ref> between German [[Bundesliga]] clubs [[Borussia Dortmund]] and [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]. In the first all-German Champions League final, Bayern won the match 2–1 with goals from [[Mario Mandžukić]] and man of the match [[Arjen Robben]] coming either side of an [[İlkay Gündoğan]] penalty for Dortmund.

One week later, Bayern won the [[2013 DFB-Pokal Final|2012–13 DFB-Pokal]] and, having already won the [[2012–13 Bundesliga]], completed the [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]]. As a result of their Champions League win, Bayern qualified to play against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], the winners of the [[2012–13 UEFA Europa League]], in the [[2013 UEFA Super Cup]], and also earned the right to enter the semi-finals of the [[2013 FIFA Club World Cup]] as the [[UEFA]] representative. They would eventually go on to win both competitions.
[[File:Le quintuplé de 2013.jpg|thumb|322x322px|The 5 trophies won by Bayern Munich in 2013 (Bundesliga, Champions League, DFB-Pokal UEFA Super Cup, FIFA Club World Cup)]]
A viewership of 21.6 million on the German [[ZDF]] and over 360 million worldwide has been reported, making it the most watched sports broadcast of 2013.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.bvb.de/ger/News/Uebersicht/Rekordquote-fuer-das-ZDF-Finale-war-meistgesehene-Sportsendung-weltweit | title=Rekordquote für das ZDF - Finale war meistgesehene Sportsendung weltweit | language=de | trans-title=Record ratings for ZDF - Finale was the most watched sports broadcast worldwide | website=www.bvb.de}}</ref>

==Background==
This was the first time in the history of the Champions League (and European Cup) that the final was contested between two German sides. There were three previous Champions League finals between two clubs from the same country: [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]] (Spain), [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]] (Italy), and [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008]] (England).<ref>{{cite web |title=Finals take domestic rivalries to international stage |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0209-0e9f7462adcb-70ab4221d22e-1000--finals-take-domestic-rivalries-to-international-stage/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 May 2013 |access-date=23 May 2013 }}</ref>

It was Bayern Munich's 10th European Cup/Champions League final, third all-time behind [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] (12) and [[A.C. Milan]] (11). They won four of those finals: in [[1974 European Cup final|1974]], [[1975 European Cup final|1975]], [[1976 European Cup final|1976]], and most recently in [[2001 UEFA Champions League final|2001]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/ucl/2013/e/e_2009612_pk.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130612062343/http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/ucl/2013/E/E_2009612_pk.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=12 June 2013|title= Match Press Kit|date=24 May 2013|work=[[UEFA]]|access-date=24 May 2013 }}</ref> The 2013 final was Bayern's third final in four years; they lost in both [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]] and [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012]] (as the first team since 1984 to reach the Final held in their home stadium), adding to previous final losses in [[1982 European Cup final|1982]], [[1987 European Cup final|1987]], and [[1999 UEFA Champions League final|1999]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Bayern's final pedigree |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0209-0e848f4fd51e-74684f3cb573-1000--bayern-s-final-pedigree/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 May 2013 |access-date=23 May 2013 }}</ref>

For Dortmund, this was their second Champions League final, with them winning their first title in [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]]. The next season as defending champions, they defeated Bayern in their only previous meetings in European competitions, winning 1–0 on aggregate in the [[1997–98 UEFA Champions League]] quarter-finals, before being eliminated 2–0 on aggregate in the semi-finals by Real Madrid, managed at the time by [[Jupp Heynckes]].<ref>{{cite web |title=German rivals face Wembley showdown |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d7f0df28034-69b2ed3c3d23-1000--german-rivals-face-wembley-showdown/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 May 2013 |access-date=23 May 2013 }}</ref>

In 2003, Bayern provided a €2 million loan without collateral to the nearly bankrupt Dortmund which has since been repaid.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blogs.independent.co.uk/2012/05/16/everyone-will-be-crossing-their-fingers-for-bayern-munich-and-so-they-should/ |title='Everyone will be crossing their fingers for Bayern Munich' – and so they should |work=The Independent |date=16 May 2012 |access-date=20 May 2012 |first=Kit |last=Holden |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120519023147/http://blogs.independent.co.uk/2012/05/16/everyone-will-be-crossing-their-fingers-for-bayern-munich-and-so-they-should/ |archive-date=19 May 2012 }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/news/20120206/bayern-dortmund/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120207210025/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/news/20120206/bayern-dortmund/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=7 February 2012 |title=Bayern helped Dortmund avoid bankruptcy with loan |magazine=Sports Illustrated |date=6 February 2012 |access-date=20 May 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/news/87/germany/2012/02/06/2889828/uli-hoeness-bayern-saved-dortmund-from-bankruptcy-with-2-million- |title=Uli Hoeness: Bayern saved Dortmund from bankruptcy with 2 million euro loan |work=Goal.com |date=6 February 2012 |access-date=20 May 2012 |first=Enis |last=Koylu }}</ref> There has been a heated rivalry between Bayern and Dortmund, known in Germany as ''[[Der Klassiker]]'', which became prevalent during the 1990s. In 2011–12, Dortmund won the [[2011–12 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] and the [[2012 DFB-Pokal Final|DFB Pokal]] with Bayern finishing runners-up in both competitions; Dortmund clinched the league title in a home match where bananas were tossed at Bayern goalkeeper [[Manuel Neuer]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ca.sports.yahoo.com/blogs/soccer-dirty-tackle/borussia-dortmund-mascot-pretended-urinate-bayern-munich-bus-050611057.html|title=Borussia Dortmund's mascot pretended to urinate on Bayern Munich's bus|website=Yahoo! Sports|date=13 April 2012 }}</ref> In 2012–13, Bayern bested Dortmund for both trophies, as well as the [[2012 DFL-Supercup|DFL-Supercup]]. Just before Dortmund's Champions League semi-final, it was announced that one of their homegrown stars, [[Mario Götze]], would make a €37&nbsp;million transfer to Bayern for the upcoming 2013–14 season,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/apr/23/borussia-dortmund-mario-gotze-transfer |title=Borussia Dortmund feel hurt by Mario Götze transfer to Bayern Munich |work=The Guardian |date=23 April 2013 |access-date=11 July 2013 |first=Sid |last=Lowe }}</ref> a move that some felt would have put more distance between wealthy Bayern and the rest of the Bundesliga.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2012/0000256619.php |title=Transfer coup of the season: Mario Götze |website=bundesliga.com |date=6 June 2013 |access-date=11 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130712120309/http://www.bundesliga.com/en/liga/news/2012/0000256619.php |archive-date=12 July 2013 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://hereisthecity.com/2013/05/30/the-mario-gtze-transfer-a-stark-realisation-about-the-state-of-t/ |archive-url = https://archive.today/20130616235637/http://hereisthecity.com/2013/05/30/the-mario-gtze-transfer-a-stark-realisation-about-the-state-of-t/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=16 June 2013 |title=The Mario Götze transfer: A stark realisation about the state of the Bundesliga |website=hereisthecity.com |date=30 May 2013 |access-date=11 July 2013 |first=Naveenan |last=Thanendra }}</ref> The final 2012–13 Bundesliga game between the two clubs was a 1–1 draw marked by acrimony as Bayern's [[Rafinha (footballer, born 1985)|Rafinha]] was sent off for elbowing Dortmund's Jakub Blaszczykowski, sparking an argument on the touchline between Dortmund coach [[Jürgen Klopp]] and Bayern sporting director [[Matthias Sammer]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2013/may/18/mario-gotze-borussia-dortmund-bayern-munich |title=Mario Götze's move from Borussia Dortmund to Bayern Munich adds spice |work=The Guardian |date=25 May 2013 |access-date=11 July 2013 |first=Marcus |last=Christenson }}</ref> The press has used terms like 'power shift' and 'changing of the guard' after Dortmund and Bayern eliminated Spanish giants Real Madrid and Barcelona, respectively, in the Champions League semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.sport360.com/football/dortmund-bayern-european-footballs-new-%E2%80%98der-klassiker%E2%80%99 |title=Dortmund-Bayern European football's new 'Der Klassiker' |website=sport360.com |date=25 May 2013 |access-date=11 July 2013 |first=James |last=Piercy |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304083436/http://sport360.com/football/dortmund-bayern-european-footballs-new-%E2%80%98der-klassiker%E2%80%99 |archive-date=4 March 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://prosoccertalk.nbcsports.com/2013/05/24/champions-league-final-preview-a-bitter-rivalry-with-much-higher-stakes/ |title=Champions League final preview: A bitter rivalry with much higher stakes |website=Pro Soccer Talk |date=24 May 2013 |access-date=11 July 2013 |first=Kyle |last=Bonne }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Wembley Stadium, illuminated.jpg|thumb|[[Wembley Stadium]] has hosted the European Cup/Champions League final seven times, most recently in 2013 and 2024.]]

Wembley Stadium, England's [[national stadium]] and home of the [[England national football team]], was announced as the venue of the 2013 final on 16 June 2011.<ref>{{cite web|title=2013 final: Wembley Stadium |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/stadium/index.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=6 May 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130530124501/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season%3D2013/final/stadium/index.html |archive-date=30 May 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Having hosted the [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011 final]], Wembley made history for being the first stadium in the tournament's history to host the final twice in three years.<ref>{{cite news|title=Wembley to host UEFA Champions League Final 2013|url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Wembley/NewsandFeatures/2011/2013-CL-160611|work=[[The Football Association]]|publisher=TheFA.com|date=16 June 2011|access-date=18 August 2011}}</ref> Discussing the short time between the two finals, [[UEFA]] President [[Michel Platini]] explained that the final would be in celebration of 150 years of the [[The Football Association|Football Association]]'s existence.<ref>{{cite news|title=Wembley, Amsterdam ArenA, Prague get 2013 finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0250-0c50f416ad08-db9153f27513-1000--wembley-amsterdam-arena-prague-get-2013-finals/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 June 2011 |access-date=16 June 2011 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110618072033/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/aboutuefa/organisation/executivecommittee/news/newsid%3D1644074.html |archive-date=18 June 2011 }}</ref> It was the seventh occasion that Wembley hosted the final after hosting the [[1963 European Cup final|1963]], [[1968 European Cup final|1968]], [[1971 European Cup final|1971]], [[1978 European Cup final|1978]], [[1992 European Cup final|1992]] and the [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011]] finals of Europe's premier club competition.<ref>{{cite web |title=Wembley's magnificent seventh |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/previousfinalsincity/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130322073419/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/previousfinalsincity/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=22 March 2013 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=1 May 2013 }}</ref>

The [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|original Wembley Stadium]] hosted five European Cup finals. The [[1968 European Cup final|1968]] and [[1978 European Cup final|1978]] finals were both won by English sides: [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] beat [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] 4–1 in 1968 and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] defeated [[Club Brugge KV|Club Brugge]] 1–0 in 1978. Benfica also lost in the [[1963 European Cup final|1963 final]], beaten 2–1 by [[A.C. Milan]], while [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] won the first of three consecutive European Cups at Wembley in [[1971 European Cup final|1971]], beating [[Panathinaikos F.C.|Panathinaikos]] 2–0. In the [[1992 European Cup final|1992 final]], Spanish club [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] defeated Italian side [[UC Sampdoria|Sampdoria]] 1–0 in the final match played as the European Cup prior to the following season's introduction of the current Champions League format.

First opened for the [[British Empire Exhibition]] in 1923, the stadium was originally known as the Empire Stadium. That year, it hosted its [[1923 FA Cup Final|first FA Cup Final]], when almost 200,000 spectators attempted to watch the match between [[Bolton Wanderers F.C.|Bolton Wanderers]] and [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]]. Wembley played host to all of [[England national football team|England]]'s matches at the [[1966 FIFA World Cup]], including the 4–2 victory over [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] in the [[1966 FIFA World Cup Final|final]], and at [[UEFA Euro 1996]]. The original stadium was closed in 2000 and demolished three years later, to be replaced by a 90,000-capacity stadium, which opened in 2007.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wembley returns to centre stage |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01d5-0e70c9e75e03-e82d6a4e7427-1000--wembley-returns-to-centre-stage/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 January 2009 |access-date=24 March 2010 }}</ref> The new stadium hosted the [[2011 UEFA Champions League final]], which pitted [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] against [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in a re-match of the final played two years previously. Barcelona claimed their fourth European title as they ran out 3–1 winners.

