==Pre-match==

===Ambassador===
The two-time Champions League winner and ex-England international [[Steve McManaman]] was appointed as the official ambassador for the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ambassador: Steve McManaman |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/ambassador/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120902231457/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/ambassador/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2 September 2012 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=1 May 2013 }}</ref>

===Officials===
In May 2013, Italian [[referee (association football)|referee]] [[Nicola Rizzoli]] was selected to supervise the final. He was joined by compatriots Renato Faverani and Andrea Stefani as [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]], [[Gianluca Rocchi]] and [[Paolo Tagliavento]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Additional assistant referee|additional assistant referees]], Gianluca Cariolato as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fifth official and reserve assistant referee|reserve assistant referee]], and Slovenian [[Damir Skomina]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth official]].<ref name="referee"/>

===Opening ceremony===
[[File:Eröffnungszeremonie Wembley 2013.jpg|thumb|left|The 2013 Champions League final opening ceremony.]]

The [[opening ceremony]] was directed by [[Kevin Spacey]]'s executive assistant and [[The Prince's Trust]] ambassador Hamish Jenkinson and his business partner Jonny Grant, who had previously directed the 2011 opening ceremony, also at Wembley. The ceremony, also titled "Battle of Kings", was produced by Films United. It was directed and produced by [[Grigorij Richters]] and his producing partner Alex Souabni. Both were the former filmmakers-in-residence of [[Kevin Spacey]] and Jenkinson at [[The Old Vic Theatre]] in [[London]]. The film focused around the story of [[activist]] and [[fundraiser]] [[Stephen Sutton]], who was part of the group of performers.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.caboodlecollective.com/sites/default/files/Irresistible%20Case%20Study.pdf |title=Irresistible Films Case Study |access-date=14 June 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714120031/http://www.caboodlecollective.com/sites/default/files/Irresistible%20Case%20Study.pdf |archive-date=14 July 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pandemoniumdrummers.co.uk/2013/05/champions-league-final-2013-at-wembley.html|title=Champion's League Final 2013 at Wembley Stadium}}</ref>

===Match ball===
The match ball for the final was the Adidas Finale Wembley, which featured the same "Starball" panel configuration as the last three finals. The ball was predominantly white, with each star in blue with yellow patterning and a purple border. Six of the 12 stars featured designs recalling the six previous times that the European Cup final had been held at Wembley Stadium. The ball was unveiled on 30 January 2013 and was used for all knockout stage matches in the 2012–13 competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Final match ball marks past Wembley showpieces |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0205-0e13e5e621da-9b449b2d14bd-1000--final-match-ball-marks-past-wembley-showpieces/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 January 2013 |access-date=26 May 2013 }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
[[File:BVB-Fans in Wembley.jpg|thumb|Borussia Dortmund fans in Wembley before kick-off]]

The international ticket sales phase for the general public ran from 11 February to 15 March 2013.<ref>{{cite web |title=Champions League final tickets: London 2013 |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/tickets/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121214164251/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/tickets/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 December 2012 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=1 May 2013 }}</ref> Tickets were available in four price categories: [[Pound sterling|£]]330, £230, £140, and £60. Due to the high demand for tickets, allocation was determined by a lottery.<ref>{{cite news |title=UEFA Champions League final ticket sales over |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0207-0e83e836b498-5ed43ed3190a-1000--uefa-champions-league-final-ticket-sales-over/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=15 March 2013 |access-date=26 May 2013 }}</ref>

The two finalist clubs were allocated 25,000 tickets each. Borussia Dortmund received 502,567 requests for tickets,<ref name=borussiatickets>{{cite news|title=BVB-Wahnsinn: 502.567 Ticketanfragen für Wembley|url=https://www.kicker.de/bvb-wahnsinn_502-567-ticketanfragen-fuer-wembley-585792/artikel|access-date=6 May 2013|newspaper=kicker|date=6 May 2013|language=de}}</ref> while there were approximately 250,000 orders for tickets from members of Bayern Munich.<ref name=bayerntickets>{{cite news|title=German footie fans bid for Wembley CL tickets|url=http://www.thelocal.de/sport/20130507-49577.html|access-date=7 May 2013|newspaper=[[The Local]]|date=7 May 2013}}</ref> Both clubs used draws as a means of awarding tickets.<ref name=borussiatickets/><ref name=bayerntickets/>

===Related events===
As is the annual custom, the UEFA Champions League and UEFA Women's Champions League trophies were presented to the host city at a special ceremony at [[Banqueting House, Whitehall]], on 19 April 2013. Receiving the trophies from UEFA President [[Michel Platini]] were the [[Minister for Sport and the Olympics]] [[Hugh Robertson (politician)|Hugh Robertson]] and the [[Mayor of London]]'s commissioner for sport, [[Kate Hoey]]. Representing the previous season's competition winners were [[John Terry]], [[Frank Lampard]], [[Petr Čech]], [[Fernando Torres]] and [[Branislav Ivanović]] of [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], and [[Lotta Schelin]] of the [[Olympique Lyonnais (Ladies)|Lyon]] ladies' team. Also in attendance were final ambassadors [[Graeme Le Saux]] and [[Faye White]], who had escorted the trophies from [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] to Banqueting House via London's public transport system, and [[David Bernstein (executive)|David Bernstein]], the chairman of [[The Football Association]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Andrew |last=Haslam |title=Champions League trophies handed to London |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea90f5e213f-c3d9a5a8d00c-1000--champions-league-trophies-handed-to-london/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=19 April 2013 |access-date=26 May 2013 }}</ref>

The UEFA Champions Festival was held at the International Quarter, [[Stratford City]], next to the [[Olympic Park, London|Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park]], from 23 to 26 May 2013.<ref>{{cite web |title=Home of the London Games hosts 23–26 May event |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/championsfestival/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130311160256/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2013/final/championsfestival/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=11 March 2013 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=1 May 2013 }}</ref>

The [[2013 UEFA Women's Champions League final]] was held at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] on 23 May 2013 between [[VfL Wolfsburg (women)|Wolfsburg]] and [[Olympique Lyonnais (ladies)|Lyon]]. Wolfsburg won the game, with [[Martina Müller (footballer)|Martina Müller]] scoring the only goal. This ensured that for the first time ever the same nation won both the men's and women's European club titles in the same year.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/womenschampionsleague/news/0252-0ce4323e5dd3-e3a5721214ac-1000--wolfsburg-restore-german-dominance/|title=Wolfsburg restore German dominance|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=24 May 2013}}</ref>

===Team selection===
Borussia Dortmund's [[Mario Götze]], who would join Bayern Munich in the summer, missed the match with a hamstring injury that had kept him out since the semi-final second leg against Real Madrid. Right-back [[Łukasz Piszczek]] did play, however, despite being due for hip surgery, while centre-back [[Mats Hummels]] recovered from a sprained ankle in time to play.<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League: Dortmund confident Mats Hummels will be fit |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22594080 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=20 May 2013 |access-date=27 May 2013 }}</ref> Bayern Munich were without defender [[Holger Badstuber]], who was expected to be out for 10 months with a knee injury,<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League final: Mario Gotze ruled out with injury |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/22540468 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=23 May 2013 |access-date=23 May 2013 }}</ref> and [[Toni Kroos]], who had yet to recover from an injury sustained in the quarter-final.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/news/champions-league-final-preview-borussia-115600896--sow.html|title=Champions League Final Preview: Borussia Dortmund – Bayern Munich|publisher=[[Yahoo!|Yahoo! Sports]]|date=24 May 2013|access-date=30 May 2013|archive-date=5 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305073156/http://sports.yahoo.com/news/champions-league-final-preview-borussia-115600896--sow.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
Dortmund were the dominant team in the first half-hour of the match, pressing Bayern intensely. [[Manuel Neuer]] made five important saves in the first 35 minutes, including two from shots by [[Robert Lewandowski]] and one from [[Marco Reus]]. Despite this dominance, Dortmund failed to generate many dangerous attacks, as Neuer's only difficult save came when [[Jakub Błaszczykowski]] volleyed a cross toward Neuer's near post, which the Bayern keeper deflected out for a corner. A [[Mario Mandžukić]] header forced a save from [[Roman Weidenfeller]] and [[Javi Martínez]] put a header from the ensuing corner just over the bar. The first half was an open affair; while Dortmund dominated the early stages, Bayern were still able to generate several chances through [[Arjen Robben]], who forced Weidenfeller into three saves in the first half. Dortmund were able to generate many of their chances due to uncharacteristically poor play from [[Philipp Lahm]], who gave the ball back to Dortmund in his own half on two occasions.<ref name=bbcreport/>

Bayern later took the initiative and scored the first goal in the 60th minute, when Robben and [[Franck Ribéry]] combined to set up Mandžukić for a left-footed finish, the ball going past [[Marcel Schmelzer]] on the goal line from three yards out.<ref name=bbcreport/> Shortly after, [[Dante (footballer)|Dante]] raised his foot in the penalty area and caught Reus in the stomach; [[İlkay Gündoğan]] scored the resulting penalty, shooting low into the left corner and sending Neuer the wrong way. Bayern had a chance to re-take the lead several moments later, when [[Thomas Müller]] rounded Weidenfeller and attempted to find Robben, with the ball rolling towards the open goal, only for [[Neven Subotić]] to make a last ditch clearance off the line, preventing a tap-in from Robben.<ref name=bbcreport/> Late in the match, Bayern seemed the more likely to score, as [[David Alaba]] forced Weidenfeller into a save with a shot from long range. Moments later, a through-ball from Robben caught the Dortmund defence cold, resulting in Bayern having Müller and Mandžukić in a two-on-one with Weidenfeller. Subotić again came to Dortmund's rescue, as he was able to catch up and pressure Müller into playing a poor pass, which left Mandžukić with a tight angle, and the Croatian blasted into the side netting. With a minute left in normal time, Ribéry played in Robben with a back-heeled pass; the Dutch forward burst past the defence and scuffed a weak, low shot past the onrushing Weidenfeller with his left foot from eight yards out for the winning goal.<ref name=bbcreport>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Borussia Dortmund 1–2 Bayern Munich |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/22540468 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=25 May 2013 |access-date=26 May 2013 }}</ref>

